WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Evidence preservation in cloud environments includes audit logs, identity activity,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:11.940
API calls, storage access, snapshots, configuration history, alert records, tickets,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.960
timelines, and responder notes.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.300
Some evidence is controlled by the customer, some by the provider,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.480
and some by SaaS vendors or identity providers.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.740
Knowing where evidence lives and how long it is retained is essential before an incident

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.840
occurs.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.620
Chain of custody at a high level means documenting what was collected, when, by whom,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.160
from where, and how it was preserved.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.460
Not every incident becomes a legal matter,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.000
but evidence handling should still be disciplined.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.380
Clean documentation supports technical analysis, management decisions,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.640
compliance obligations, insurance questions, and possible customer communications.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.780
Communication must be coordinated.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.520
Technical responders, service owners, business leaders, legal counsel, privacy teams,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.300
compliance teams, customer support, and cloud providers may all have roles.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.040
Early messages should distinguish confirmed facts from uncertainty.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.740
Overstating conclusions can damage trust,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.040
while withholding necessary coordination can slow containment and recovery.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.300
After-action reviews turn incidents into improvement.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.920
A good review asks what happened, how it was detected, what worked,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.940
what slowed the response, what evidence was missing, what controls failed,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.920
and what should change.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.660
The purpose is not blame.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.400
The purpose is to improve detection, runbooks, architecture, identity controls, logging,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.260
training, and escalation paths.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.560
Lessons learned should become tracked work.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:47.020
Update runbooks, tune detections, improve asset inventory, change risky defaults,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.320
strengthen access controls, adjust retention, and rehearse the new process.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:56.420
A cloud incident response program matures when every incident produces better preparation

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.760
for the next one.
